This is the perfect time to level up. You have mastered One-to-One (1 Parent = 1 Child).
Now, we move to One-to-Many.
 * Concept: One Parent has A List of Children.
 * Real World Example: One Amazon Order contains Many Products.
🍔 Project: "The Food Delivery App" (Swiggy/Zomato Clone)
The Scenario:
You are building the "Checkout" system.
 * The Input: A user places One Order that contains a list of items (e.g., 2 Burgers, 1 Coke, 1 Fries).
 * The Logic Challenge: You must automatically calculate the Total Bill Amount before saving.
 * The Output: Return the Order ID and the Total Price.
1️⃣ The Data Structure (Visualizing the JSON)
Unlike the Passport project where you had flat fields, here you will receive a List (Array) of items.
Input (What comes from the Frontend):
{
    "customerName": "Rahul",
    "deliveryAddress": "Mumbai",
    "items": [
        { "foodName": "Chicken Burger", "price": 150.0 },
        { "foodName": "Coke", "price": 50.0 },
        { "foodName": "Fries", "price": 80.0 }
    ]
}

Output (What you return):
{
    "orderId": 101,
    "customerName": "Rahul",
    "totalAmount": 280.0,  <-- You calculated this! (150+50+80)
    "status": "PREPARING"
}

2️⃣ The Entities (The Database Design)
You need two tables.
Class A: FoodItem (The Child)
 * Simple class.
 * Fields: id, foodName, price.
 * Important: It needs to link back to the Order.
Class B: FoodOrder (The Parent)
 * Fields: id, customerName, deliveryAddress, totalAmount.
 * The Relationship: Instead of private FoodItem item; (One-to-One), you need a LIST:
   @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
@JoinColumn(name = "fk_order_id") // Creates the foreign key in the Child table
private List<FoodItem> items; 

3️⃣ The Logic Challenge (Service Layer) 🧠
Your createOrder method will be harder this time. You have to loop through the list.
The Algorithm:
 * Create the Parent: Make a new FoodOrder object.
 * Handle the Children:
   * The Request DTO has a List<ItemRequest>.
   * You need to loop through this list (using a for loop).
   * For every item in the list, create a new FoodItem entity.
   * Add this entity to a new Java List (e.g., ArrayList<FoodItem>).
 * The Calculation:
   * While looping, add up the prices (total = total + item.getPrice()).
   * Set this final total in the Parent (order.setTotalAmount(total)).
 * The Link:
   * order.setItems(itemList);
 * Save:
   * orderRepo.save(order); (This saves the Order AND all 3 food items instantly).
📝 Your Task Checklist
 * Create DTOs:
   * OrderRequest (Needs a List inside it!).
   * OrderResponse (Simple summary).
 * Create Entities:
   * Use @OneToMany in the Parent.
   * Use List<FoodItem> in the Parent.
 * Write the Service:
   * Use a for loop to process the items and calculate the total price.
This is a significant jump in complexity because of the List.
Take your time. Start by designing the Entities and show them to me if you get stuck on the List syntax. 🚀
